# Allowlist, not denylist.
#
# RedLens's denylist failed exactly the way denylists fail: `vsce ls` showed the
# package would ship personal email addresses, lab hostnames, the built test
# harness and raw TypeScript. Every new folder in the repo would have had to be
# remembered here, forever, by someone who was not thinking about packaging.
#
# So: exclude everything, then add back only what is loaded at runtime. A new
# folder is invisible to the package by default, which is the only default that
# fails safe.
#
# Verify after ANY change with:  npx @vscode/vsce ls
# And after packaging:           scripts/ci/audit-vsix.sh base <vsix>

**

# --- required by the Marketplace ---
!package.json
!README.md
!LICENSE.md
!CHANGELOG.md
!NOTICES.md

# --- loaded at runtime ---
# Named precisely, NEVER with a folder wildcard. In vsce a `!` rule beats every
# exclusion regardless of order, so `!dist/**` would re-include source maps
# permanently and no later line could take them back out. A .map re-exposes the
# entire source; for the paid half that is the whole of the protection.
!dist/extension.js
!dist/mcp-server.js
!media/icon.png
